00:39:30.980 --> 00:39:41.140
And so for the, the, uh, it has the virtues and then it has truth, love, courage, and then the color of, uh, I think it's the color of their stone.

00:39:41.900 --> 00:39:43.160
Oh yeah. It's the stones.

00:39:43.580 --> 00:39:45.080
And, but that's left blank too.

00:39:45.240 --> 00:39:48.100
Intentionally. So you have to kind of like solve that for like, that's what those colors mean.

00:39:48.100 --> 00:40:04.400
And the truth, love, and courage of it, like, that's very interesting too, because that, that's not explained, but each one of these virtues also like falls into, uh, this truth, love, courage triangle where like they, some are, some are truth and love.

00:40:04.560 --> 00:40:05.620
Some are love and courage.

00:40:05.660 --> 00:40:07.240
Some are courage and truth.

00:40:07.520 --> 00:40:08.680
Like some are just truth.

00:40:08.840 --> 00:40:10.240
It's, it's crazy.

00:40:10.420 --> 00:40:17.900
Uh, but you have to understand that to, to, and you know, it sounds like, again, that just sounds like a philosophy course.

00:40:17.900 --> 00:40:28.760
Like you just have to, you know, you can't, you can't take your best guess, but in a, in a certain sense, you do need to just use some reasoning to understand this stuff too, by what the, the world tells you.

00:40:29.180 --> 00:40:33.340
Yeah. But you need that stuff to finish the, the later half of the game.

00:40:33.340 --> 00:40:38.440
So you are kind of putting together a puzzle with the assistance of the manual here as well.

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:40.060
For those things.

00:40:40.240 --> 00:40:44.380
Yes. But for something as simple as like, where are the moon gates?

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:45.900
They just tell you.

00:40:46.560 --> 00:40:49.340
Yeah. I mean, they also just tell you sometimes about hidden rooms too.

00:40:49.500 --> 00:40:54.060
Like, you know, for the maps of towns, they're like, just so you know, there are two hidden rooms in here.

00:40:54.140 --> 00:40:56.380
And it's just like, yeah, cause that was a pretty stupid thing to do.

00:40:56.940 --> 00:41:00.180
Like you shouldn't be able to walk through walls to find new stuff.

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:02.600
That shouldn't be something that we have to think about.

00:41:03.200 --> 00:41:07.760
And maybe this was just one big elaborate piece of DRM.

00:41:07.900 --> 00:41:10.040
Who knows? And then there's the dungeons.

00:41:11.500 --> 00:41:12.640
Ah, yes. Our favorite.

00:41:12.640 --> 00:41:15.000
Our favorite type of thing.

00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:20.400
First person dungeons on the NES.

00:41:20.800 --> 00:41:22.880
We've seen this with so many games.

00:41:23.480 --> 00:41:26.440
Most recently it was that Serpents game.

00:41:26.680 --> 00:41:28.540
Serpents and what was it?

00:41:28.940 --> 00:41:30.780
Serpents and what? Yeah, it was one of those.

00:41:34.720 --> 00:41:36.700
Serpents and... Slitheries.

00:41:36.700 --> 00:41:38.300
No, it was... Slitheries. Serpents was the end.

00:41:38.540 --> 00:41:39.540
Serpents was the end of it.

00:41:39.660 --> 00:41:41.220
It was like... Super Serpents.

00:41:41.740 --> 00:41:42.700
Yeah, Super Serpents.

00:41:42.740 --> 00:41:44.820
No, it was Swords and Serpents.

00:41:44.820 --> 00:41:45.900
Swords and Serpents.

00:41:47.280 --> 00:41:50.420
It was, you know, a game we didn't really care for.

00:41:50.540 --> 00:41:52.640
But some people have written in to say it's a pretty good game.

00:41:52.640 --> 00:41:53.760
Or at least an interesting game.

00:41:53.940 --> 00:41:56.920
I think the problem is I'm just never going to care for this kind of exploration.

00:41:58.240 --> 00:42:01.780
And it remains very punishing in Ultima.

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:05.880
I've just decided I don't like mazes, especially in first person.

00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:09.200
And I think that just goes beyond video games.

00:42:09.320 --> 00:42:14.680
Like if you put me in a labyrinth, I would not have fun.

00:42:14.680 --> 00:42:26.800
Like I don't want to go into a hedge maze on some like rich person's property or a fall exhibition or anything.

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:28.540
Like just don't put me in mazes.

00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:36.760
Yeah, and I think the problem is, again, with these mazes is that not even that it can become aimless.

00:42:36.880 --> 00:42:39.420
I think like there is some focus here.

00:42:39.420 --> 00:42:45.100
It's not like a labyrinth of floors and traps and stuff.

00:42:45.100 --> 00:42:51.060
But there still is like walls that you can walk through and, you know, and shit like that where it's just like, please.

00:42:51.340 --> 00:42:58.660
Like we're not there yet as a society where we can spend this kind of time figuring this stuff out.

00:42:58.720 --> 00:43:03.260
Like why do they – I don't know why they make it that hard.

00:43:03.260 --> 00:43:04.660
Like that's just me personally.

00:43:04.820 --> 00:43:10.920
I'm caving here and saying like that's the kind of stuff that tells me I'm not going to be able to play this game.

00:43:11.320 --> 00:43:16.200
And maybe that's just such a weird line to draw on the NES library.

00:43:16.480 --> 00:43:22.080
But like that's the kind of stuff that for me just isn't even fun because it's going to require –

00:43:22.080 --> 00:43:23.780
Now here's what bothers me.

00:43:23.800 --> 00:43:24.920
It's not even that it's that hard.

00:43:24.920 --> 00:43:38.460
Like maybe the first time in your entire RPG playing career you came across a false wall and your mind was blown by it.

00:43:38.540 --> 00:43:40.300
It was like holy shit this is a giant secret.

00:43:40.620 --> 00:43:43.340
Never would have even thought something like this could exist in a game.

00:43:44.080 --> 00:43:45.200
So wow.

00:43:45.480 --> 00:43:47.860
You did it. False walls are a thing.

00:43:48.360 --> 00:43:49.780
And then it became a trope.

00:43:49.780 --> 00:44:00.060
And now not only is it the trope of the false wall but it's the only thing that they can think of to spice up a maze.

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:12.560
Like how about some other kind of puzzle that requires some like just contextual thinking and not let me just bang my head up against every wall I see.

00:44:12.720 --> 00:44:17.840
Be like oh maybe I should take this rock and put it with these other rocks in this like triangle of rocks.

00:44:17.960 --> 00:44:19.760
I don't know. Something more interesting.

00:44:20.320 --> 00:44:21.580
Like oh how do I unlock this door?

00:44:21.700 --> 00:44:24.300
Maybe there's like this thing I can pull in another room.

00:44:24.460 --> 00:44:25.360
Like just do that.

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:28.540
Like not everything has to be invisible walls.

00:44:28.700 --> 00:44:29.580
And I'm done with my rant.

00:44:30.080 --> 00:44:31.100
And unfortunately like – One trick pony.

00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:32.880
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:44:32.980 --> 00:44:39.940
One trick pony sure. But also like unfortunately this is the actual like locking to the end of the game.

00:44:39.960 --> 00:44:42.220
It is completing these dungeons.

00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:45.360
You know, master the eight virtues or not.

00:44:45.520 --> 00:44:54.500
If you don't collect the runes and stones of these virtues then you're not going to be able to complete the game.

00:44:54.500 --> 00:45:01.860
And so this again it's like you can be an honest, compassionate, sacrificial person with a lot of humility.

00:45:02.200 --> 00:45:09.780
But it's not going to matter if you can't actually play the first person dungeon parts of the game.

00:45:09.780 --> 00:45:16.880
And I guess I would say like if you're willing to master the virtues you're probably going to also overcome these dungeons.

00:45:18.900 --> 00:45:24.920
Which, you know, I guess what I'm laying down is that it's just a very large commitment.

00:45:25.600 --> 00:45:26.980
One way or the other.

00:45:26.980 --> 00:45:32.280
This isn't the kind of game that like you can be like, you know what?

00:45:32.580 --> 00:45:34.080
I'll focus on honesty.

00:45:34.260 --> 00:45:36.040
It's like it's just not going to work out for you, bud.

00:45:36.240 --> 00:45:41.500
You know, like you got to be all in on the world and its systems.

00:45:41.900 --> 00:45:47.240
And I just – I think it's such a shame that we're getting such a cool – such a cool game.

00:45:47.560 --> 00:45:50.980
Such a radically thinking game for the time.

00:45:51.480 --> 00:46:00.240
And then like this type of thing feels like it gets like abandoned or relegated strictly to the PC going forward.

00:46:00.580 --> 00:46:04.360
Whereas like technology did get better on consoles too.

00:46:04.620 --> 00:46:13.940
And you just wish that like we know that certain things in these RPGs can be streamlined where you don't have to have a talk button.

00:46:13.940 --> 00:46:17.760
You don't have to have – you know, we saw it in Dragon Warrior like a stairs button, right?

00:46:17.760 --> 00:46:26.280
It's like as these things got better, it's kind of weird that we never got like a more streamlined version of these types of games.

00:46:26.540 --> 00:46:31.000
Oh, we did. But like what would you – I guess like what would you say though?

00:46:31.100 --> 00:46:34.400
Like still with this kind of scope, we got something of this scale?

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:36.440
No, I mean it was going to be a joke.

00:46:36.780 --> 00:46:42.480
Like a streamlining of – because again, like I do want to give this game credit where it's due.

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:57.480
This whole morality system is more complex than anything until probably the last like six, seven years in terms of like quote unquote morality systems.

00:46:57.660 --> 00:47:00.100
Like then we saw it again like Bioshock.

00:47:00.160 --> 00:47:02.540
Like are you going to kill the girl or are you going to save the girl?

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:07.620
Like it was just very binary and it's like are you going to be good or are you going to be evil?

00:47:07.620 --> 00:47:11.440
Where this is like are you going to be honest or are you going to be a liar?

00:47:11.600 --> 00:47:15.720
Like are you going to be compassionate or are you going to be cold?

00:47:16.060 --> 00:47:18.440
Like there's so much more going on here.

00:47:18.760 --> 00:47:27.340
And that was what I was going to jokingly say is like yes, we got a streamlined version of morality later in video games.

00:47:27.700 --> 00:47:31.180
But not in any actual sense.

00:47:31.180 --> 00:47:44.360
But you also have to think and I'm totally making a stretch here but like Ultima 4 taking a swing like this into what even an RPG could be about.

00:47:44.640 --> 00:47:47.100
You know like not having to have some big bad or anything.

00:47:47.360 --> 00:47:51.800
Kind of like leads eventually into even games like Fallout, right?

00:47:51.800 --> 00:47:58.320
Other CRPGs to probably take inspiration from this of on a different scale of course.

00:47:58.560 --> 00:48:05.740
But taking these systems that were laid down so early and not quite refined yet and expanding them even larger.

00:48:06.180 --> 00:48:07.500
Yeah. No, for sure.

00:48:07.700 --> 00:48:18.020
And you know the whole Bethesda stuff with like Morrowind and even well I guess even before that with Arena and Daggerfall.

00:48:18.020 --> 00:48:28.820
Like yeah, this is in terms of just scope, open-endedness, that this definitely set the bar for the whole genre.

00:48:30.480 --> 00:48:33.640
But yeah, it's not without its pitfalls.

00:48:33.920 --> 00:48:44.320
After you have truly maxed all eight virtues, you have to get the, there's like a three-part key that you have to assemble.

00:48:44.320 --> 00:48:48.980
You have to have the bell of courage, the book of truth, the candle of love.

00:48:50.300 --> 00:48:58.660
And then once you have all eight runes and all eight virtue stones, I don't even know how to say this, the great Stygian abyss.

00:48:58.860 --> 00:49:00.700
You have to like get to the entrance of that.

00:49:00.940 --> 00:49:10.100
Stygian. Which is a feat in and of itself because it's also, I think this is where those pirates are.

00:49:10.780 --> 00:49:17.600
And so you're doing a bunch of boat battling and you have to get your, you know, your ship ready to take them on.

00:49:17.600 --> 00:49:28.640
But you descend through the abyss, which again leads to a multi-floor dungeon crawl, of which of course there are monsters and traps.

00:49:28.820 --> 00:49:32.680
But keep in mind, like there's no fast travel back out of there either.

00:49:32.860 --> 00:49:35.660
You know, like you made the trek just to get to the abyss.

00:49:35.660 --> 00:49:42.780
You have to be incredibly well equipped for this encounter because it's a long, it's a long journey.

00:49:43.040 --> 00:49:44.640
It was a long journey to get there too.

00:49:44.980 --> 00:49:48.520
It's not like there's some healer hanging out outside.

00:49:48.720 --> 00:49:50.860
It's like, yeah, just come back up out here and I'll heal you.

00:49:50.860 --> 00:50:04.300
And then once you get to like the final chamber or whatever, that is where again, you know, you have to prove your understanding of the eight virtues.

00:50:04.440 --> 00:50:08.560
It's more of like another morality test, only this time with actual answers.

00:50:09.720 --> 00:50:19.820
And that's where the significance of the eight comes into play again, Sean, because the final answer is, is you have to turn that eight sideways to reveal that.

00:50:20.020 --> 00:50:22.780
Infinity? The answer to everything is infinite.

00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:25.800
That's so cool, dude.

00:50:26.160 --> 00:50:28.120
It's so cool, dude.

00:50:28.340 --> 00:50:30.200
And let me tell you, it actually is cool.

00:50:30.400 --> 00:50:44.720
Like, I feel like that's the kind of thing that if you played this game for a hundred hours and you finally came to that moment where you realized you just have to, you know, flip that eight or turn it or turn it 90 degrees.

00:50:44.940 --> 00:50:48.820
I feel like that would actually unlock a very proud moment in someone's head.

00:50:48.820 --> 00:50:51.560
And if assuming, of course, that they were able to come to that conclusion on their own.

00:50:51.960 --> 00:50:53.160
I agree.

00:50:53.720 --> 00:50:59.640
I think that it's just not the right time in my life to be playing this game to really appreciate it.

00:50:59.960 --> 00:51:04.500
And that's a great point, too, Sean, because because that you're right.

00:51:04.500 --> 00:51:14.080
It's not. But in 1990, theoretically, only like people like us were the only ones who could have played a game like this.

00:51:14.700 --> 00:51:26.420
What 12-year-old is getting their hands on Ultima 4 and jiving with it as opposed to playing like, you know, we just had we've had Super Mario Brothers 3.

00:51:26.420 --> 00:51:27.700
We've had Mega Man 3.

00:51:27.880 --> 00:51:28.960
We've had Castlevania 3.

00:51:29.100 --> 00:51:32.520
It's like those are established franchises on the console they love.

00:51:32.520 --> 00:51:33.760
That's where they're going to be.

00:51:34.000 --> 00:51:36.520
I'm not doubting that people own this game.

00:51:36.700 --> 00:51:39.380
But do you really think kids were putting up with this system?

00:51:39.520 --> 00:51:42.040
It was clearly like 30-year-old men loved that.

00:51:42.040 --> 00:51:46.240
30. That's a good point.

00:51:46.520 --> 00:51:53.100
I imagine we'll get some comments that said that I was the 12-year-old that was really into this.

00:51:53.280 --> 00:51:55.840
Well, I could imagine you being the 12-year-old to the listener.

00:51:55.840 --> 00:51:57.100
I'm going to talk to you directly.

00:51:57.100 --> 00:52:01.860
I could imagine you being the 12-year-old and being into this and it being even formative.

00:52:02.260 --> 00:52:06.560
But I'm not I'm going to be a little skeptical if you told me you beat the game.

00:52:06.820 --> 00:52:08.000
I'm going to be skeptical.

00:52:08.620 --> 00:52:16.340
It's funny that I think you mentioned something along the lines at the very beginning of the episode, too, that I read.

00:52:16.460 --> 00:52:18.940
I don't know if it's true or not, because it was on the Wikipedia.

00:52:19.420 --> 00:52:30.760
But it was like Richard Garriott was when this game shipped, he was the only one to have beaten it, which doesn't bode well for its quality assurance.

00:52:32.720 --> 00:52:41.100
But it very much is is fitting for a game like this that the only guy that finished it was the guy that designed and wrote the whole thing.

00:52:41.100 --> 00:52:54.700
And if you think about where Ultima was beforehand, the first Ultima, I mean, which is so early 1981, it's incredibly impressive, even just that it was made that in wizardry were like the RPGs at all in general.

00:52:55.800 --> 00:53:01.960
And that one even like tried a wild swing of having space combat inside it.

00:53:02.040 --> 00:53:06.080
So it wasn't even just like, you know, you know, at the end of the game, it's like we're going to space like that's cool.

00:53:06.720 --> 00:53:07.640
That's something different.

00:53:07.640 --> 00:53:12.080
And then the second one was like, no, if you don't forget space, it's all about time travel now.

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:15.900
So, all right. You got, you know, you got me interested there.

00:53:16.100 --> 00:53:20.480
The third one, forget about you're not just a singular hero anymore.

00:53:20.620 --> 00:53:21.440
Now you have a party.

00:53:22.400 --> 00:53:31.460
And that was the one that also introduced the tactical battle shift instead of, you know, instead of just having one on one battles.

00:53:32.600 --> 00:53:35.160
And now it's like, well, forget it.

00:53:35.160 --> 00:53:40.200
Like, forget everything you knew about Ultima because now we're doing a morality system.

00:53:40.620 --> 00:53:47.600
And, you know, there's still that dungeon crawl part of the game and everything.

00:53:47.600 --> 00:53:53.920
But I feel like this is such a big swing that love it or hate it.

00:53:54.120 --> 00:53:55.620
Like, you got to respect it.

00:53:55.920 --> 00:53:56.760
Of course.

00:53:57.520 --> 00:53:59.400
Yeah. Where it goes from here.

00:53:59.900 --> 00:54:02.500
I actually don't think we get any of these on the NES.

00:54:02.760 --> 00:54:04.960
I don't think we get Ultima 5, 6, or 7.

00:54:06.100 --> 00:54:08.020
But I'll cover them real quick.

00:54:08.720 --> 00:54:14.080
Ultima 5 introduces a time of day system where NPCs have daily schedules.

00:54:14.520 --> 00:54:17.680
So, so much for Shenmue being the first thing to do that.

00:54:18.680 --> 00:54:21.060
Any chance I can get the dunk on Shenmue, I will.

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:32.300
The conversation depth still has the keyword based conversation style where you just have to, you know, figure out what the clue is to get them to keep talking.

00:54:33.660 --> 00:54:36.060
And by clue, I mean, like, they mention a town.

00:54:36.260 --> 00:54:38.300
And so then you shoot that town back at them.

00:54:38.380 --> 00:54:39.300
And they're like, oh, of course. Yeah.

00:54:39.360 --> 00:54:40.300
Let me tell you more about it.

00:54:40.560 --> 00:54:43.880
Can you flirt? You know, they didn't mention any romances.

00:54:44.220 --> 00:54:45.640
So you'll have to wait for fable.

00:54:45.820 --> 00:54:47.180
I'm not even talking about romance.

00:54:47.280 --> 00:54:49.480
I just mean, like, flirting with the NPCs.

00:54:49.480 --> 00:54:53.160
Yeah, maybe there is a thing where, like, if you, you know, you say you look cute.

00:54:53.380 --> 00:54:55.180
But remember, it's only the first four letters.

00:54:55.480 --> 00:54:57.700
So they're not going to, they're just going to say you look.

00:54:58.000 --> 00:54:58.860
Just say cute. You look.

00:55:00.200 --> 00:55:01.640
Cute. And they'll be like, oh, thanks.

00:55:02.040 --> 00:55:07.700
Yeah. But then Ultima 6, what's the difference here?

00:55:08.040 --> 00:55:14.720
It's a, it's, my notes say that there's more object manipulation.

00:55:15.100 --> 00:55:16.120
That's not a good note.

00:55:16.340 --> 00:55:17.380
I don't know what that means.

00:55:18.720 --> 00:55:19.860
It's more object manipulation.

00:55:20.040 --> 00:55:22.920
I guess maybe more like you can interact with more things in the world.

00:55:23.060 --> 00:55:27.920
Like maybe if there's like a fireplace, you can interact with it instead of it just being

00:55:27.920 --> 00:55:28.900
part of the set design.

00:55:29.100 --> 00:55:30.440
Yeah. Flush the toilets is great.

00:55:30.580 --> 00:55:31.960
You could do that in Deja Vu as well.

00:55:32.320 --> 00:55:33.720
Oh, yes. That's true.

00:55:34.200 --> 00:55:35.780
Yeah. You can do that in Deja Vu as well.

00:55:36.320 --> 00:55:37.560
Ah, yes. That's true.

00:55:37.560 --> 00:55:44.380
And Ultima 7 is completely, I mean, now we're in 1992.

00:55:44.820 --> 00:55:46.060
That's Underworld, right?

00:55:46.720 --> 00:55:48.380
What's that? Is that Underworld?

00:55:49.000 --> 00:55:50.340
It's called the Black Gate.

00:55:51.000 --> 00:55:52.840
Hmm. Maybe it is.

00:55:52.840 --> 00:55:54.820
And that one is now fully mouse driven.

00:55:55.660 --> 00:55:58.440
You can drag and drop things on screen.

00:55:58.440 --> 00:56:00.860
So it's no longer heavy reliance on the keyboard.

00:56:03.480 --> 00:56:07.140
And it introduced graphical pop-up interfaces.

00:56:07.580 --> 00:56:08.560
What the hell? Pop-up pads?

00:56:09.080 --> 00:56:10.440
Yeah. You know, it's just funny.

00:56:10.580 --> 00:56:13.820
It's like, I guess like the NES technically had that already.

00:56:14.040 --> 00:56:16.500
Like we already had tons of graphical pop-ups.

00:56:16.600 --> 00:56:18.120
We have little text boxes and stuff.

00:56:18.120 --> 00:56:24.360
I guess in the computer versions of these games, they were still being like relegated to the right side of the UI.

00:56:24.780 --> 00:56:26.120
And they were finally like, hey, you know what?

00:56:26.300 --> 00:56:27.160
Here's a concept.

00:56:27.400 --> 00:56:30.760
Windows. And they had to pay Bill Gates like $3 billion.

00:56:33.000 --> 00:56:36.120
Did you ever think about Windows like that growing up?

00:56:36.380 --> 00:56:39.160
That Windows was about the Windows that you had?

00:56:39.460 --> 00:56:41.480
Yes. Did you know that?

00:56:41.780 --> 00:56:45.820
I wouldn't say that I knew that when I was a kid.

